  "worked_solution": "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+Q\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-P{e}^{\\pi } & ={e}^{\\pi } \\\\ P & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+Q\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2P{e}^{\\pi }-2Q{e}^{\\pi } & =4{e}^{\\pi } \\\\ -2P-2Q & =4 \\\\ 2-2Q & =4 \\\\ Q & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-1$ and $Q=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x-\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
